WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.000
Film Courage: What are you learning&nbsp;
inside of these [writing] programs?

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:06.640
Niceole R. Levy, Screenwriter/Author: They all are&nbsp;
structured a little bit different. I would say in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:11.440
general the big takeaways are first of all you're&nbsp;
going to write a new piece of material and you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:16.400
going to do it with professional mentorship. This&nbsp;
is going to be getting notes from people who give&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:21.920
studio and network notes to real professional&nbsp;
writers all the time. It's a little bit different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:26.080
than your writer's group giving you notes or your&nbsp;
friends and it gives you a sense of what that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:30.320
like when you're on staff, when you're developing&nbsp;
to get notes from those kind of professionals.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:37.040
Hopefully you have a great new piece of material&nbsp;
to help you secure representation and to go out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:44.720
for staffing. The programs will also prep you&nbsp;
for staffing in terms of executives coming in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:49.600
and meeting with you and liking you. They'll&nbsp;
consider sending your name to their showrunners&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:55.840
that they're working with who are looking for new&nbsp;
writers. Also you get practice in how to handle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.840 --> 00:01:00.960
meetings with showrunners, how to go through a&nbsp;
general meeting because like I always tell people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:07.280
when I got in the CBS program I would have treated&nbsp;
a showrunner meeting like a regular job interview&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:12.880
because I had no idea what it was. It is very&nbsp;
much not that. It is really just go in and have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:19.040
a conversation with someone because the truth of a&nbsp;
meeting a general meeting or a showrunner meeting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:26.320
is that they already like your writing. That's&nbsp;
how you got in the room. Now you're there to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:33.360
show who you are to be your best self to make them&nbsp;
laugh if you're doing a comedy to tell interesting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:39.920
stories. If you're doing a drama that's tied to&nbsp;
your experience whatever it is and show them that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:45.200
basically you're a nice normal person you're not&nbsp;
going to make their life harder and they wouldn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:51.760
mind being in a room with you for 10 12 hours a&nbsp;
day and that's half the trick is just being able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:58.400
to go in and be relaxed and do those meetings and&nbsp;
so that's a big element of almost every program&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:04.400
is teaching you how to handle that that prep and&nbsp;
how to get ready for those meetings what are the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:11.360
key benefits that TV writing programs offer I&nbsp;
mean first of all it's that that experience of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:17.200
meeting some executives meeting some managers to&nbsp;
get practice for how to do those kind of meetings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:24.640
and also introductions right I got my first agent&nbsp;
through an introduction from one of the programs I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:29.600
got my manager through an introduction from one of&nbsp;
the programs so that helps you find representation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.600 --> 00:02:35.600
if you don't have it I met tons and tons of&nbsp;
executives some of whom have become close friends&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:41.360
over the years from that those writing programs&nbsp;
so you have that connection base that you start&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:47.520
to build out it gives you a cohort of writers to&nbsp;
be your support system as you build your career&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:54.000
because you need people to if something crazy&nbsp;
happens in the room you need someone to be like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:59.120
on on your break texting going what is this&nbsp;
craziness that just happened is it just me or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:03.680
is this crazy and they can be like nope that's&nbsp;
crazy just remember take a deep breath it's all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:11.120
going to be fine or to get advice on things&nbsp;
when you're you're stuck on a story point&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.120 --> 00:03:15.680
and you're scared to go to the showrunner and&nbsp;
you can have a friend who's like no it's better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:19.600
go ask the sharing before you make&nbsp;
a mistake in the script like do it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:27.280
those kind of things and then it's also just&nbsp;
the the community that you start to build I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.160 --> 00:03:34.800
hate networking it's like one of my least favorite&nbsp;
words I used to hate when they would be like we're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:41.360
having a writer's mixer and I would have to go&nbsp;
and full disclosure I still hate the idea of them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.920 --> 00:03:47.360
what has happened over the years though is that I&nbsp;
would force myself to go I would be like just go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:53.440
for an hour just go for one hour and then talk to&nbsp;
a few people and you can leave and because I have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.440 --> 00:03:59.600
done that because the writing programs so often&nbsp;
co-mingle and have crossover you develop this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.600 --> 00:04:04.000
whole network of people that you are friendly with&nbsp;
and who you like and who are interesting to talk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:10.640
to and you build this whole community so when one&nbsp;
of my friends becomes a showrunner and it's like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.640 --> 00:04:17.440
I need a great BIPOC mid-level writer who do we&nbsp;
all can respond to that because they just reach&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.440 --> 00:04:21.600
out to their people and then we all are like&nbsp;
oh this person this person's great I'll have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:26.480
I'll get a script and send it to you and we can&nbsp;
support each other and help each other as we all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.480 --> 00:04:31.360
grow through our careers so that's honestly one&nbsp;
of the most valuable things that comes out of it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.080 --> 00:04:38.000
and you're kind of with them for life so&nbsp;
to speak a little bit yeah it's like the my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:44.560
class of from CBS and from NBC we're all&nbsp;
still in touch we're all still very close&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.280 --> 00:04:54.400
we celebrate each other's wins we commiserate&nbsp;
over our losses it's it's it's your little TV&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.400 --> 00:04:58.800
family that you build and then as you build your&nbsp;
career every room you go to you're going to meet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:04.400
people who you want to know for the rest of your&nbsp;
life it won't be everyone but there's going to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.400 --> 00:05:09.840
people in every room that you're&nbsp;
like now you are stuck with me

00:05:10.880 --> 00:05:17.040
do you think you would have broken into television&nbsp;
had you not gone through one of these programs I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.040 --> 00:05:22.640
don't think I would have to be honest I think I&nbsp;
had tried everything I could think of outside of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.640 --> 00:05:30.480
the programs and the one thing I had not been able&nbsp;
to do was work as an assistant in television and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.480 --> 00:05:36.000
because I put myself through undergrad and&nbsp;
graduate school and had very hefty student&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:42.240
loans I just couldn't afford to I did not have the&nbsp;
support system to allow me to make what assistance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:49.120
make and then also keep a roof over my head and so&nbsp;
that was just never going to be a thing I could do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:55.120
and even when I tried to do it people were&nbsp;
like you have a master's degree you cannot be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.120 --> 00:06:01.120
a PA [production assistant] and I was like no no I&nbsp;
can I really can trust me I will make it work and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.120 --> 00:06:07.920
they were like no we're not gonna we don't believe&nbsp;
you so that opportunity wasn't there and I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.920 --> 00:06:13.120
were it not for the programs i'm not sure how I&nbsp;
would have ever made it happen I mean I would have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.120 --> 00:06:20.240
continued to write certainly would it have been&nbsp;
enough to finally get a door to open I don't know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.240 --> 00:06:24.880
had you been tempted to leave your master's&nbsp;
degree or even your bachelor's off a resume&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.880 --> 00:06:30.400
so there's some survival jobs here in la they&nbsp;
pay some pretty good wages do you think they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.400 --> 00:06:37.680
felt you were overqualified and it scared them&nbsp;
away yeah yeah absolutely and and for me like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.280 --> 00:06:44.800
the job that I was doing the day job as much&nbsp;
as I hated it it allowed me to survive and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.800 --> 00:06:49.200
it allowed me to pay all my bills and pay&nbsp;
on my loans and keep a roof over my head&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.760 --> 00:06:52.960
and so it was just like look this&nbsp;
is the way it's going to be and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.520 --> 00:07:00.880
it could have there it was that push-pull of&nbsp;
yes I could have taken the master's degree off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.880 --> 00:07:05.760
my resume but so much of what I had learned&nbsp;
about how the business worked I had learned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.760 --> 00:07:10.560
because of that master's program because&nbsp;
this was before the writing fellowships right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.560 --> 00:07:17.680
so I was also cutting down the sense of&nbsp;
experience I had so it was like i'm just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.680 --> 00:07:23.360
gonna try it and they'll say yes or they'll say no&nbsp;
and they always said no but i'm still here anyway&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.200 --> 00:07:28.800
what advice do you have to other&nbsp;
writers who are considering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:35.040
these kinds of programs my biggest advice is when&nbsp;
you're when you're thinking about what to submit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.760 --> 00:07:41.680
whether it's a spec or a pilot I think CBS might&nbsp;
be the only program left or Viacom might be the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.680 --> 00:07:48.160
only program left that's requiring specs much to&nbsp;
my dismay but whatever it is you're submitting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.960 --> 00:07:56.240
if you were going to be identified by one piece of&nbsp;
writing that's the script you submit because you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.240 --> 00:08:02.480
only really have that one shot and then the&nbsp;
next year if you didn't get into any of them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.480 --> 00:08:09.120
you have to have the next script that is the&nbsp;
script that you would want to represent you so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.120 --> 00:08:13.600
that's why I say you always have to be&nbsp;
writing because you always need new material&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.160 --> 00:08:18.400
even once you start your career people&nbsp;
will have read the two samples that you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.040 --> 00:08:25.360
broke in with and so you have to have new samples&nbsp;
to send out places so always be writing and when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.360 --> 00:08:31.760
you get to the points in those applications where&nbsp;
they ask you there's always either essay questions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.760 --> 00:08:38.960
or a video element and it's like what do you&nbsp;
have to contribute to a writer's room really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.960 --> 00:08:47.120
think about what your life uniquely offers to your&nbsp;
storytelling it's not about how much you love TV&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.120 --> 00:08:52.160
it's not about how you've dreamed of being a&nbsp;
writer since you were a kid it's about I have gone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.160 --> 00:08:58.880
through x y and z in my life and not trauma just&nbsp;
experiences you've been through it can be that you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.880 --> 00:09:05.760
you lived in 17 places because your dad was in the&nbsp;
military or that you love to hike and you've hiked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.760 --> 00:09:10.320
seven of the craziest places in the world to&nbsp;
hike whatever it is that's special about you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.120 --> 00:09:16.720
you want to bring out in those questions and that&nbsp;
response because that's the stuff that people are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.720 --> 00:09:23.120
looking for it's like what's the special sauce&nbsp;
you bring into the room because almost everyone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.120 --> 00:09:27.520
and I do say almost because not a blanket&nbsp;
statement almost everyone who wants to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.520 --> 00:09:35.840
a TV writer loves TV so it's like why you and&nbsp;
that's the question you're really answering.

